---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Eyebrow from '../components/Eyebrow.astro';
import CopyButton from '../components/CopyButton.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, ''); // e.g. "/harnessed.dev"

const installCmd = "set -g @plugin 'harnessed-ai/tmux-nudge'";

const footerLinks = [
  { href: 'https://github.com/harnessed-ai/tmux-nudge', label: 'tmux-nudge' },
];
---
<Base
  title="tmux-nudge"
  current="projects/tmux/nudge"
  githubHref="https://github.com/harnessed-ai/tmux-nudge"
  footerLinks={footerLinks}
>
  <section style="padding: 96px 0 48px;">
    <Eyebrow>tmux-nudge</Eyebrow>
    <h1 style="font-family: var(--font-heading); font-weight: var(--font-heading-weight); font-size: clamp(36px, 4.6vw, 60px); line-height: 1.1; letter-spacing: -0.015em; margin: 0; margin-left: -0.05em; max-width: 22ch;">The pane that needs you, blinking</h1>
    <p style="font-size: 17px; line-height: 1.65; max-width: 62ch; margin: 24px 0 0; color: var(--color-text);">
      A tmux plugin that nudges you when a pane needs a human — by blinking a label on the pane and its entry in the status bar. Anything can nudge you: a finished shell script, an idle build, or an AI coding agent waiting for your response.
    </p>
    <div style="display: flex; gap: 12px; flex-wrap: wrap; margin-top: 28px;">
      <a href="https://github.com/harnessed-ai/tmux-nudge" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn btn-primary" style="white-space: nowrap;">View on GitHub ↗</a>
      <a href="#install" class="btn btn-secondary" style="white-space: nowrap;">Install</a>
    </div>
    <figure style="margin: 44px 0 0;">
      <img src={`${base}/nudge-demo.gif`} alt="tmux-nudge blinking a label on a pane and its status-bar entry while a pane waits for a human, then auto-clearing when viewed" loading="lazy" width="960" height="581" style="display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; border: 1px solid var(--color-divider); border-radius: var(--radius-md); box-shadow: var(--shadow-md);" />
      <figcaption style="margin-top: 12px; font-size: 13px; color: var(--color-text); opacity: 0.6;">A pane demanding attention — blinking until you look, then auto-clearing.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <hr class="hr" />

  <section style="padding: 48px 0;">
    <h2 style="font-family: var(--font-heading); font-weight: var(--font-heading-weight); font-size: 26px; margin: 0 0 14px;">Why</h2>
    <p style="font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.65; max-width: 64ch; margin: 0; color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-text) 78%, transparent);">
      Generic completion notifiers only know "the process stopped" — they can't tell done from waiting for input, and modern AI-agent TUIs redraw constantly, so silence/activity monitoring misfires on them. AI-agent indicators read hooks and get precise state, but are AI-only, use static border colours, and can't style individual panes independently. tmux-nudge unifies both behind one renderer: pluggable trigger sources feed a single "make this pane demand attention" engine, so the visual treatment is identical no matter what triggered it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <hr class="hr" />

  <section style="padding: 48px 0;">
    <h2 style="font-family: var(--font-heading); font-weight: var(--font-heading-weight); font-size: 26px; margin: 0 0 24px;">Trigger sources</h2>
    <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0,1fr)); gap: 16px; margin-bottom: 32px;">
      <div class="card elev-sm">
        <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;">
          <span style="width: 8px; height: 8px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--color-accent); animation: tk-blink 1s steps(1) infinite;"></span>
          <p class="card-kicker" style="margin: 0;">AI agents</p>
        </div>
        <p class="card-body">Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode and Kiro CLI report through a shared agent hook: orange when an agent is waiting on you, green when it's done, red on an error. Other harnesses use nudge hook.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card elev-sm">
        <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;">
          <span style="width: 8px; height: 8px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--color-accent); animation: tk-blink 1s steps(1) infinite; animation-delay: 0.3s;"></span>
          <p class="card-kicker" style="margin: 0;">Shell completion</p>
        </div>
        <p class="card-body">A precmd hook in zsh/bash — any long-running command nudges its pane on completion, and is published in the shared agent state too.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card elev-sm">
        <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px;">
          <span style="width: 8px; height: 8px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--color-accent); animation: tk-blink 1s steps(1) infinite; animation-delay: 0.6s;"></span>
          <p class="card-kicker" style="margin: 0;">Manual / scripts</p>
        </div>
        <p class="card-body">nudge on flags anything from anywhere — a plain CLI, no integration required.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div style="background: var(--color-surface); border: 1px solid var(--color-divider); border-radius: var(--radius-md); padding: 18px 20px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 14px; flex-wrap: wrap;">
      <span style="font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace; font-size: 13.5px; font-weight: 600; background: #e0a940; color: #111; padding: 1px 8px; border-radius: 3px; animation: tk-blink 1s steps(1) infinite;">● waiting · fifo · Bash: npm test</span>
      <span style="margin-left: auto; font-size: 13px; color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-text) 55%, transparent);">the pane's border label names the agent and what it waits on; the window's borders and its status-bar entry light up in the same colour, until you look</span>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6; margin-top: 16px; color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-text) 62%, transparent); max-width: 64ch;">A small daemon animates the blink and auto-clears any flagged pane the moment you view it. It starts on the first nudge and exits when none remain. Windows with agents keep their label row, so panes never resize when a nudge comes and goes.</p>
  </section>

  <hr class="hr" />

  <section style="padding: 48px 0;">
    <h2 style="font-family: var(--font-heading); font-weight: var(--font-heading-weight); font-size: 26px; margin: 0 0 14px;">Other sessions</h2>
    <p style="font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.65; max-width: 64ch; margin: 0 0 20px; color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-text) 78%, transparent);">
      The blink lives in the session you're looking at. When something in another tmux session needs you, a chip appears in front of your status-right: the most urgent one elsewhere, coloured by its state, plus how many more are waiting.
    </p>
    <div style="background: var(--color-surface); border: 1px solid var(--color-divider); border-radius: var(--radius-md); padding: 14px 20px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px; flex-wrap: wrap; font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace; font-size: 13.5px; color: var(--color-text);">
      <span>1:zsh&nbsp;&nbsp;2:claude</span>
      <span style="margin-left: auto;"><span style="color: #e0a940;">●</span> infra:2 build <span style="opacity: 0.55;">+1</span></span>
      <span style="opacity: 0.55;">ghostty</span>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.6; margin-top: 16px; color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-text) 62%, transparent); max-width: 64ch;"><strong style="font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-text);">prefix + g</strong> jumps to the next pane that needs you, in any session: waiting before done, oldest first. Press it again to move on. Set @nudge-elsewhere to manual to place the chip yourself, or off to hide it; @nudge-jump-key picks another key.</p>
  </section>

  <hr class="hr" />

  <section style="padding: 48px 0;">
    <h2 style="font-family: var(--font-heading); font-weight: var(--font-heading-weight); font-size: 26px; margin: 0 0 18px;">CLI</h2>
    <pre style="background: var(--color-surface); border: 1px solid var(--color-divider); border-radius: var(--radius-md); padding: 16px 18px; margin: 0; overflow-x: auto; font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--color-text); max-width: 620px;">nudge init                      # set up the renderer; saves your config
nudge install                   # wire the shell + the shared agent hook (Claude, Codex, OpenCode, Kiro)
nudge uninstall                 # fully reverse init; restores your config
nudge on  [-t pane] [-s state]  # state: needs-input | done | error
nudge off [-t pane]
nudge sync -t pane              # redraw a pane from the shared agent state
nudge next                      # jump to the next pane that needs you (prefix + g)
nudge status</pre>
  </section>

  <hr class="hr" />

  <section id="install" style="padding: 48px 0 80px;">
    <Eyebrow>Install</Eyebrow>
    <h2 style="font-family: var(--font-heading); font-weight: var(--font-heading-weight); font-size: 26px; margin: 0 0 20px;">TPM (recommended)</h2>
    <p style="font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 16px; color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-text) 78%, transparent); max-width: 62ch;">Add one line and press prefix + I. On first load tmux-nudge also wires the shell integration and the shared agent hook for Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode and Kiro CLI, for whatever you have installed. Codex runs a new hook only once you approve it in its /hooks screen.</p>
    <pre style="background: var(--color-surface); border: 1px solid var(--color-divider); border-radius: var(--radius-md); padding: 16px 18px; margin: 0; overflow-x: auto; font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--color-text); max-width: 520px;">set -g @plugin 'harnessed-ai/tmux-nudge'</pre>
    <CopyButton command={installCmd} />

    <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0,1fr)); gap: 16px; margin-top: 32px;">
      <div class="card">
        <p class="card-kicker">Config</p>
        <p class="card-body">Colours, labels and blink interval are tmux options — set them before tmux-nudge loads; any can be overridden per-invocation with a matching NUDGE_* env var. When a pane counts as seen (@agent-seen-on: focus or prompt) is shared with the other plugins.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <p class="card-kicker">One hook, shared</p>
        <p class="card-body">Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode and Kiro report through one agent hook that the harnessed-ai plugins share. It lives at one fixed path, so each agent event runs once however many of them you install, and a Codex approval survives updates. Any other harness points its event config at "nudge hook &lt;state&gt;" — no new code.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <style is:global>
    @keyframes tk-blink { 0%, 49% { opacity: 1; } 50%, 100% { opacity: 0.25; } }
  </style>
</Base>
